{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cghy7YU2TAVJuqN9hV9p2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XMRgfjporPoJirjw4xneqX","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261104-062918__xe36ddc/work/PLAN.md","limit":260,"offset":20},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1027,"cache_read_input_tokens":22192,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1027},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"df25608b-8d30-41bd-8640-efa7a82527f1","uuid":"4016e6d7-3cc5-4b4e-b327-d84e2621ff97","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:29:33.358Z","request_id":"req_011Cghy7Y6hdthAZUKnXNaXs","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01XMRgfjporPoJirjw4xneqX":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261104-062918__xe36ddc/work/PLAN.md","limit":260,"offset":20}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XMRgfjporPoJirjw4xneqX","type":"tool_result","content":"20\t- Dev folder (all working files): `CricketKerfuffle-Cricket-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174825/`, inside the folder that holds TASK.md and this PLAN.md.\n21\t- The single-file game: `CricketKerfuffle-Cricket-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174825.html`, in that same root folder, beside TASK.md.\n22\t- `exit-interview.json`, also in that root folder, beside the .html.\n23\t- If *your* TASK.md gives a different model or timestamp suffix, use your TASK.md's suffix with the same GameTitle and SOURCE tokens.\n24\t- On screen the title is **CRICKET KERFUFFLE**. In the AI Hub catalogue it is `Cricket Kerfuffle`.\n25\t\n26\t### 1.2 Sources to build on\n27\t- **Engine:** my-3D2dge **v0.85.2**, handoff package: `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff` (commit `4390a98` when this plan was written). Copy the whole `dist/handoff/` folder into `<dev>/engine/` and treat it as yours to change (§3.5 lists the planned engine changes).\n28\t- **AI Hub:** Session Protocol v1, client **1.4.0**: `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub`. Download only the files you need with `curl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/<file>`: `README.md`, `session.js`, `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` and `examples/parallel.html`. **Don't `git clone` ai-hub:** a shallow clone took over 5 GB of disk when this plan was written.\n29\t- **Read before coding.** Read these whole: `engine/API.md`, `engine/kits/sports.js` (the closest kit: ball as a Body, aftertouch, formations, a radar, commentary on Dialog's radio, a bot), `engine/docs/ASSETS.md`, `engine/modules/arcade.js` (tables, initials, attract helpers) and the hub `README.md`. In `engine/AI_GUIDE.md`, read these sections: *Standard features*, *Frame order*, *Scenes*, *Camera*, *Players*, *Motion and timing*, *TileMap*, *Humanoid*, *Audio*, *Text and UI*, *Lighting*, *Testing without a browser* and the final *Checklist*. Search `engine/API-INDEX.md` for names; don't read it whole.\n30\t- **Machine etiquette (from TASK.md).** Other agents share the machine.\n31\t  - Read only your own folder.\n32\t  - `/tmp` may be full, so clone into `<dev>/.cache/` or `~/.cache/<unique-name>/`.\n33\t  - When a server is needed, run `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` from the folder to serve, parse the port it prints, and confirm the page is yours (fetch it and check for `<title>Cricket Kerfuffle`).\n34\t  - Stop only the processes you started, by PID. Never use pkill or killall, and never kill whatever holds a port.\n35\t\n36\t### 1.3 Ground rules that shape everything\n37\t- No TODOs, placeholders or stubs: every feature in this plan ships complete. Where the plan offers a simpler fallback (§10), the fallback must be complete too.\n38\t- All audio is synthesized in code: no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish (§5.13).\n39\t- Controller first, for a TV seen from 10 feet away (§5.14 and §5.15). Keyboard and mouse must feel native. Touch-only devices get no touch controls (§5.15.6).\n40\t- The game runs from `file://` with no network requests. The AI Hub client only talks to the hub when the hub opened the page.\n41\t- The performance budget is unlimited (TASK.md), so FPS tuning is out of scope. Still avoid pathological designs, such as re-baking the floor every frame (§5.11).\n42\t\n43\t---\n44\t\n45\t## 2. The game in brief\n46\t\n47\t| | |\n48\t|---|---|\n49\t| Genre | Arcade sports (cricket), over the top, with a comedy story campaign |\n50\t| Perspective | 2.5D in the engine's projected-3D views. A **broadcast camera** (TV-style, behind the bowler, looking down the pitch at the batter) for every delivery. A high **wide camera** that follows the ball round the ground. A side-on **review camera** for third-umpire replays and a **close-up camera** for celebrations. Story scenes use the three-quarter view. |\n51\t| Players | 1 to 4 on one machine: versus, co-op and 2v2, all on one shared screen. 1 to 4 on separate devices through AI Hub sessions (*Super Over Showdown*, parallel mode). |\n52\t| Tone | Village cricket's gentle eccentricity: tea, cake, drizzle, the vicar as umpire, a cow at deep midwicket. Delivered in the voice of the cricket commentary box (digressive, plummy, dry, given to long tangents) and of Wisden-style match reports. Then turned up to arcade bombast: flaming bats, triple-ball bowling machines, sixes through the pub window. |\n53\t| Session | Story campaign of about 40 minutes (5 chapters). A Quick Match takes 4 to 12 minutes. A Super Over Showdown takes 2 minutes. |\n54\t\n55\t**What the player can do:**\n56\t- Bat: aim with the stick or mouse; play a ground, lofted or defensive shot, or leave; charge down the pitch; use a signature special.\n57\t- Run: call runs, send the partner back, dive for the crease.\n58\t- Bowl: pick 1 of 3 deliveries or a special; place the pitching marker; time the release meter; add aftertouch swing or drift; choose the field; bowl over or round the wicket.\n59\t- Field: chase with the active fielder, dive, take catches on a timing ring, throw to either end for run-outs.\n60\t- Review an umpire's decision once per innings.\n61\t- Pick up arcade items, smash stadium targets, survive chaos events, go ON FIRE.\n62\t\n63\t**What the player can't do:** walk freely around the ground, edit teams or players, or play innings longer than 5 overs (no Test matches). In story mode the player always captains Kerfuffle CC. Each screen's help and the Nets mode make these limits clear.\n64\t\n65\t**Modes:** Story (*The Grand Kerfuffle*), Quick Match, Super Over Showdown (bat or bowl; high-score tables), Nets (practice, lessons and the \"Six Machine\" challenge), Versus (local 2–4), AI Hub session (parallel Super Over), plus Attract mode, Dev mode and About/Credits.\n66\t\n67\t---\n68\t\n69\t## 3. Architecture\n70\t\n71\t### 3.1 Stack\n72\t- **my-3D2dge v0.85.2**, the full engine (`engine/my-3d2dge.js`, every module built in). Use these parts:\n73\t  - `E.Game` scenes, boot steps, the standard pause menu, settings, controls, full screen and saves;\n74\t  - `E.TileMap` for the ground;\n75\t  - custom `E.View`s;\n76\t  - `E.Humanoid` rigs, `E.Attack` specs and `E.ACTIONS`;\n77\t  - `E.Body`, particles, lights and weather;\n78\t  - `E.Dialog`, `E.Menu`, `E.font`, `E.ui`;\n79\t  - `game.script`, `game.tween`, `E.Path`;\n80\t  - the `arcade` module (`E.Arcade` tables and initials), the `story` module (`E.Story` for cutscene data) and the `songs` module (jingles);\n81\t  - the chip synth `game.audio`, plus our own Web Audio voice synth on the engine's audio context.\n82\t- **AI Hub session client 1.4.0**, pasted unmodified.\n83\t- **Node 18+** for the build and the headless tests. **Playwright 1.63.0** (pinned by `engine/package.json`) for the browser checks and the AI Hub harness.\n84\t- Nothing else. No libraries, no CDN, no fetch.\n85\t\n86\t### 3.2 Folder layout (inside `<dev>`)\n87\t```\n88\tengine/                 copy of dist/handoff (modified: see 3.5 and engine/CHANGES-KERFUFFLE.md)\n89\tvendor/aihub/           session.js (unmodified), harness.html, bridge.js, round.js, README.md\n90\tsrc/                    the game: plain script fragments, concatenated in name order into one IIFE\n91\t  00-head.js            'use strict'; const E = My3D2dge; shared helpers (clamp, lerp, ...)\n92\t  01-const.js           units, geometry, enums (4)\n93\t  02-tuning.js          TUNING registry: defaults, difficulty multipliers, live get/set, persistence (5.19)\n94\t  03-rng.js             seeded streams: play, cosmetic, hub (3.4)\n95\t  04-bus.js             event bus: on(type, fn), emit(type, data); a 200-entry event log for dev mode\n96\t  05-store.js           profile: campaign progress, stars, unlocks, settings mirror, high-score ids\n97\t  06-assets-store.js    the few store entries used, cut by engine/tools/asset-set.mjs (6.3)\n98\t  07-assets-kerfuffle.js our new shapes and materials, as a standalone set file (6.5)\n99\t  10-data-teams.js      teams and players (8.2)\n100\t  11-data-grounds.js    ground recipes (8.3)\n101\t  12-data-deliveries.js delivery catalogue (8.4)\n102\t  13-data-shots.js      shot catalogue and the resolver table (8.5)\n103\t  14-data-specials.js   signature specials (8.6)\n104\t  15-data-arcade.js     items, stadium targets, chaos events (8.7)\n105\t  16-data-story.js      chapters, scenes, beats, goals (8.8)\n106\t  17-data-lines.js      commentary lines, match-report templates, UI copy (8.9)\n107\t  18-data-music.js      songs and stings (8.10)\n108\t  20-ball.js            ball physics and the trajectory predictor (5.1)\n109\t  21-bowling.js         delivery choice, marker, meter, release, aftertouch (5.2)\n110\t  22-batting.js         shot resolver, timing, contact, edges (5.3)\n111\t  23-running.js         running between wickets (5.4)\n112\t  24-fielding.js        fielders, interception, catches, throws, keeper (5.5)\n113\t  25-umpire.js          decisions, LBW, run-out, stumping, review (5.6)\n114\t  26-rules.js           match state, laws, scoring, scorecard (5.7)\n115\t  27-ai.js              CPU batting, bowling, captain, fielding, autopilot (5.8)\n116\t  28-hype.js            Hype meter, ON FIRE, specials runtime (5.9)\n117\t  29-chaos.js           items, targets, chaos events, weather, time of day (5.10)\n118\t  30-ground.js          TileMap builder from a ground recipe, floor texture, stands, crowd, panoramas (6.3)\n119\t  31-cast.js            rig factory: kits, gear, bat, faces, portraits (6.4)\n120\t  32-anim.js            pose library: stance, shots, bowling action, fielding, umpire signals (7)\n121\t  33-fx.js              trails, flames, stumps and bails, splinters, fireworks, confetti (6.6)\n122\t  34-camera.js          views and the camera director (5.11)\n123\t  35-replay.js          recorder, Owl-Eye replay, third-umpire screen (5.11)\n124\t  40-audio.js           SFX voices, crowd engine, music glue, stings (5.13)\n125\t  41-voice.js           gibberish speech synth (5.13.3)\n126\t  42-commentary.js      commentary director and captions (5.12)\n127\t  43-glyphs.js          button and key icons, device-following prompts (5.14.3)\n128\t  44-hud.js             match HUD (5.15.2)\n129\t  45-ui.js              10-foot widgets: big menus, cards, panels, sliders (5.15)\n130\t  50-title.js 51-attract.js 52-front.js (main menu, setup, versus lobby) 53-story.js (map, cutscenes)\n131\t  54-match.js (gameplay scene) 55-results.js 56-nets.js 57-showdown.js 58-about.js\n132\t  60-hub.js             AI Hub session flow (5.17)\n133\t  70-dev.js             dev panel (5.19)\n134\t  80-debug.js           game.debug: snapshot, force*, bot hooks (11.2)\n135\t  99-start.js           new E.Game(...), scenes, boot steps, game.start\n136\ttools/build.mjs         concatenate src -> dist/game.js; write dist/index.html (dev page) and the single-file game\n137\ttools/serve.mjs         start python3 http.server on port 0 at 127.0.0.1; return { port, pid, stop() }\n138\ttest/run.mjs            THE one test command (11.1)\n139\ttest/suites/*.mjs       the suites\n140\ttest/hub/               harness files + the built game, served for the harness run\n141\tdist/                   game.js, index.html (loads ../engine/my-3d2dge.js and game.js: quick iteration)\n142\tDESIGN-NOTES.md         short: how to add a team, a ground, a chapter, a delivery, a shot, a line\n143\t```\n144\t\n145\t### 3.3 How the code is organized\n146\t- **Simulation and presentation are separate.** The match simulation lives in `20–29`. It is pure and deterministic: fixed steps of 1/120 s, seeded RNG, no drawing and no audio. Presentation lives in `30–45`: it reads the simulation's state and listens on the event bus. Tests drive the simulation headless. The attract mode and the AI drive it through the same input interface a player uses.\n147\t- **Registries.** All content is data keyed by id: teams, players, grounds, deliveries, shots, specials, items, targets, events, chapters and lines. Systems look things up by id, and content never edits system code. Adding a ground means a new recipe in `11-data-grounds.js` plus any new shapes. `DESIGN-NOTES.md` says how, for each kind of content.\n148\t- **Event bus.** The rules emit events: `ball:released`, `ball:pitched`, `bat:contact {timing, shot}`, `ball:edge`, `ball:beaten`, `runs {n}`, `boundary {4|6}`, `wicket {how}`, `catch:dropped`, `appeal`, `decision {out, review}`, `extra {kind}`, `over:end`, `innings:end`, `match:end {result}`, `hype:full`, `fire:on|off`, `special {id}`, `item {id}`, `target:hit {id}`, `chaos {id}`, `rain:start|end`, `review:start|result`, `milestone {fifty|hattrick|maiden}`. Commentary, crowd, HUD, camera, Hype, achievements and the coverage tracker (§11) all subscribe.\n149\t- **TUNING.** One registry of the live gameplay variables. Systems read `T('ballSpeed')` at each use and never cache it, so dev sliders take effect at once.\n150\t- **The delivery director.** One state machine runs each ball (§5.7.3): `setup → runup → release → flight → inPlay → dead → between`. Each state has explicit exits and a timeout, so play can never stall.\n151\t- **Scenes** (`game.go(name, data)`): `title`, `attract`, `front` (main menu, mode select, setup, versus lobby), `storymap`, `cutscene`, `match` (pausable), `results`, `nets` (pausable), `showdown` (pausable), `about`, `hubcard` and `hubround` (pausable). Story, Quick Match, Showdown, Nets and the attract mode all run inside the `match` scene machinery, using a `MatchConfig` (§5.7.1).\n152\t\n153\t### 3.4 Determinism and randomness\n154\t- `RNG.play`: gameplay randomness (seam, AI errors, fielding). Seeded per match from `MatchConfig.seed`.\n155\t- `RNG.cosmetic`: crowd, particles and commentary-line choice, so gameplay never depends on them.\n156\t- In AI Hub sessions, the over's deliveries come from `AIHub.rng('over')` and each ball's fielding stream from `AIHub.rng('field' + n)`, so every copy faces identical balls whatever the player does (§5.17).\n157\t- Never call `Math.random` in simulation code. It is fine for engine-internal cosmetics.\n158\t\n159\t### 3.5 Planned engine changes\n160\tMake each change in `engine/my-3d2dge.js`. Log every one in `engine/CHANGES-KERFUFFLE.md`; About/Credits and the exit interview report them.\n161\t1. **Cricket bat:** a new Humanoid `weapon: 'bat'`. Add it next to the `o.weapon === 'sword'` branch in the HD draw, which uses `ops.push({ d, f })` with `J.handR`, `J.bladeDir` and `o.bladeLen`. It is a flat willow blade: length `o.batLen` (default 15 units: 5 handle, 10 blade), width 1.7 units, a darker edge line, a toe, and a coloured sticker band. Grip in `colors.grip`, blade in `colors.willow`. Give it a two-handed grip: the left hand IK'd to the handle, 2.5 units above the right hand (`E.ik3` from the left shoulder), applied in the draw pose. Add a smear for swings.\n162\t2. **Cricket gear:**\n163\t   - `pads: true`: white leg guards over the shins and knees, with buckles in `colors.trim`;\n164\t   - a `brim` hat style (umpire's and sun hats);\n165\t   - `hat: { style: 'helmet', grille: true }`: a batting helmet with a face grille;\n166\t   - `gloves: 'batting' | 'keeper'` for bigger hands;\n167\t   - `face: { moustache: 'handlebar' | 'walrus' | null, beard: 'full' | 'goatee' | null, glasses: true }`.\n168\t3. **Audio voice bus:** add `_voiceBus` beside `_sfxBus` and `_musBus`, a `voiceVolume` setting in the Sound group (label \"Voices\"), and `audio.bus(name)` returning the sfx, music or voice gain node, so the game's own Web Audio nodes follow the player's volumes.\n169\t4. **Button symbols:** in the standard settings table (`glyphs`, around line 2999), set the default to `'playstation'` and the options to `[['playstation','PlayStation'],['xbox','Xbox (A B X Y)']]`.\n170\t5. **Hub's Create + Options:** while `game.hubActive` is set and Pad8 (Create) is held, Pad9 (Options) must not open the pause menu.\n171\t6. **Floor bakes:** `TileMap._floor` keeps at most 6 baked floors (it clears the cache past that). Raise the limit to 10, so the four match cameras and the story views never re-bake (§5.11).\n172\t7. Any engine bug found along the way: fix it, and add one line about it to the log.\n173\t\n174\t### 3.6 Build and bundle (`node tools/build.mjs`)\n175\t1. Concatenate `src/*.js` in name order inside `(() => { ... })();`, giving `dist/game.js`. Fail on a syntax error: run `new Function` on it in Node.\n176\t2. Write `dist/index.html`, which loads `../engine/my-3d2dge.js` and `game.js` as separate scripts (fast iteration).\n177\t3. Write the single file `../CricketKerfuffle-Cricket-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174825.html`, in exactly this order:\n178\t   - `<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\">`;\n179\t   - **the session client** (`vendor/aihub/session.js`, byte-for-byte) as the first `<script>`;\n180\t   - viewport meta, `<title>Cricket Kerfuffle</title>`;\n181\t   - the hub tags (§5.17.1) and catalogue tags `hub:type`=`Game`, `hub:players`=`1-4`;\n182\t   - the page CSS (from `engine/game/index.html`), `</head><body>`;\n183\t   - the engine's boot splash markup (from `engine/game/index.html`, title CRICKET KERFUFFLE);\n184\t   - `<canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas>`;\n185\t   - the engine inlined in a `<script>`, then `dist/game.js` inlined in a `<script>`.\n186\t   Escape any `</script>` inside the inlined code.\n187\t4. Print the size and the SHA-1 of the client block. The bundle test (§11) checks the block is identical to `vendor/aihub/session.js`.\n188\t\n189\t---\n190\t\n191\t## 4. Units, coordinates and constants (`01-const.js`)\n192\t\n193\t- **Engine convention:** 16 world units = 1 m (docs/ASSETS.md). x east (screen right), y south (screen down in yaw-0 views), z up.\n194\t- **The pitch** runs along x = 0. The **striker's stumps are at y = 0** (the far end, top of the broadcast screen). The **bowler's stumps are at y = 320** (20 m; the near end, bottom of the screen). The striker's popping crease is at y = 20 and the bowler's at y = 300. Return creases are at x = ±21. The pitch strip is 48 units wide (3 m).\n195\t- The camera always looks from behind the current bowler, so the bowling end is always at +y. At each change of ends, the batters swap logical places and the field is recomputed relative to the new striker. Visually, the \"far-end panorama\" (§6.3) switches to the other end's.\n196\t- **Stumps:** physics box x ∈ [−3, 3], z ∈ [0, 12], depth 1. Drawn slightly larger for readability: 6 units wide, 12.5 tall, with two bails.\n197\t- **Ball:** physics radius 0.6 units. Drawn as a 4×4 px sprite at minimum, with a 2 px shadow disc, a trail and a glow at night. Leather red `#b8202a` with a cream seam, white at night, pink at the Megadome.\n198\t- **Players:** Humanoid size 1.15–1.25 (about 28–30 units tall), so `E.HEIGHTS` defaults hold.\n199\t- **Batter's frame (handedness):** angles are measured from \"straight\" (toward the bowler, +y), positive toward the **off side**. A right-hander (RHB) faces −x in stance, so the off side is −x (screen left in the broadcast view). For a left-hander, everything mirrors (x → −x). Store `hand = +1` (RHB) or `−1` (LHB). The world direction for a batter-frame angle θ is `[−hand·sin θ, cos θ]`.\n200\t- **Boundary:** an ellipse per ground (rx × ry, about 560–760 units, i.e. 35–48 m) centred on the pitch midpoint (0, 160), scaled by `boundaryScale` *(T)*.\n201\t- **Physics constants:**\n202\t\n203\t  | Constant | Value |\n204\t  |---|---|\n205\t  | `gravity` *(T)* | 160 u/s² (real) |\n206\t  | `ballSpeed` *(T)* | scale 0.62 on nominal pace (a \"140 km/h\" ball travels at 386 u/s and reaches the bat in about 0.75 s) |\n207\t  | `pitchBounce` *(T)* | restitution 0.55 (times the ground's pitch factor) |\n208\t  | Outfield rolling decay | exp(−k·dt), k = 0.55 (ground factor; wet ×1.6) |\n209\t  | Grass bounce for hit balls | restitution 0.42 |\n210\t  | `runSpeed` *(T)* | 120 u/s |\n211\t  | `fieldSpeed` *(T)* | 105 u/s |\n212\t  | `throwSpeed` *(T)* | 420 u/s |\n213\t\n214\t  At these values a single takes about 2.9 s, and a fielder 30 m away gathering in 1.2 s makes a close run-out.\n215\t\n216\t---\n217\t\n218\t## 5. Systems and mechanics\n219\t\n220\t### 5.1 Ball physics and predictor (`20-ball.js`)\n221\t- **State:** `{ x, y, z, vx, vy, vz, spin (rad/s about z, signed), swingA (lateral accel), age, phase: 'held'|'delivery'|'hit'|'rolling'|'dead', pitched, bounces, lastTouch: {kind, id}, carried (hit and not yet bounced), trail[] }`.\n222\t- **Step (1/120 s), in order:**\n223\t  1. Gravity: vz −= g·dt.\n224\t  2. While `phase === 'delivery'` and not yet pitched, add swing: a lateral acceleration perpendicular to the ball's ground velocity, `swingA · min(1, age/0.35)` (late swing).\n225\t  3. Drift for spin.\n226\t  4. Drag: v ×= (1 − 0.02·dt).\n227\t  5. Integrate.\n228\t  6. Ground contact at z ≤ 0:\n229\t     - **On the pitch** (|x| ≤ 24 and y in −20..340), for a delivery: vz' = −vz · pitchBounce · ground.bounce · delivery.bounce. Horizontal speed × (0.86 − spinGrip). Turn the ground velocity by `turn° · ground.turn · T('spin')` plus the ball's stored seam deviation `ball.seamDev`. That deviation is drawn once **at release** from RNG.play (normal, σ = `seam` *(T)* · ground.seam · delivery.seam degrees) and kept on the ball. Emit `ball:pitched`.\n230\t     - **On grass** (any ball after the bat): bounce at 0.42; below 25 u/s vertically it becomes `rolling`, with decay k.\n231\t  7. Boundary check (§5.7.4).\n232\t  8. Target and item checks (§5.10).\n233\t  9. Stumps check (§5.6).\n234\t- **Solving a delivery** (`solveDelivery(release, target, kmh, deliveryType)`): given the release point (x = ±8 for over or round the wicket, y = 300, z = 34), the pitch target (tx, ty) and the speed, find the initial velocity that lands at the target *without* swing, using `E.arc`-style closed-form projectile maths with the delivery's dip. Swing and aftertouch then move the real pitching point. That is the point of swing, and what the batter must read.\n235\t- **Predictor** (`predict(ballState, seconds, opts)`): runs the same step function on a copy and returns sampled points `{t, x, y, z, phase}` every 1/120 s. It is used for:\n236\t  - the batting timing (when the ball reaches the contact plane);\n237\t  - the AI's reads;\n238\t  - fielder interception;\n239\t  - the landing point of a catch;\n240\t  - LBW (the path continued past the pad);\n241\t  - the Owl-Eye tube.\n242\t\n243\t  The step function draws no random numbers (the seam deviation is already on the ball), so the prediction equals reality exactly. The AI adds its own perception error on top.\n244\t- **Tests (unit-ball):**\n245\t  - 50 random solved deliveries without swing pitch within 1 unit of the target;\n246\t  - an outswinger to a right-hander lands on the off side of the target (−x) and an inswinger on the leg side;\n247\t  - a leg break pitched on middle turns toward −x for a right-hander;\n248\t  - the predictor matches the stepped simulation within 0.01 units over 3 s;\n249\t  - rolling balls stop;\n250\t  - a ball dropped from z = 50 bounces lower each time.\n251\t\n252\t### 5.2 Bowling (`21-bowling.js`)\n253\t**The flow for a human bowler:**\n254\t1. **Between balls**:\n255\t   - R1 (Space) cycles the field preset (Attacking, Balanced, Defensive, or Spin attack for spinners);\n256\t   - L1 (Shift) switches over or round the wicket;\n257\t   - the stick, WASD or the mouse moves the **pitch marker**: a ring on the pitch, clamped to y ∈ [20, 230] and |x| ≤ 30, with labelled length bands *yorker / full / good / short / bouncer* drawn faintly on the pitch the first few overs;\n258\t   - the delivery panel at the bottom shows the bowler's 3 deliveries on ✕ □ ○ and the special on △ (greyed out until Hype is full).\n259\t2. Pressing a delivery button starts the **run-up**: pace bowlers run 2.0 s, medium 1.5 s, spinners 0.9 s. The marker can still move during the run-up, at half speed.\n260\t3. **The release meter**: during the last 0.7 s, a needle sweeps a bar left → right → left at `meterSpeed` *(T)* (1.4 Hz). The centre zone (width `meterSweet` *(T)* 0.18 of the bar, scaled by bowler skill) is green. Press any delivery button or click (✕/J/LMB) to release:\n261\t   - **green**: accuracy scatter ±2 units, pace and spin 100–105%;\n262\t   - **amber**: scatter ±10, 90%;\n263\t   - **red edge**: scatter ±18, 80%;\n264\t   - **the far-right red tip** (the last 6%) is an **overstep**: a no-ball and a FREE HIT;\n265\t   - **no press**: auto-release at the end with an amber result.\n266\t4. **Aftertouch**: for 0.25 s after release, the stick's horizontal push adds swing (seamers) or drift (spinners), up to the delivery's allowance ±0.6. The mouse's horizontal movement does the same.\n267\t5. **Specials** (△ when Hype is full) skip the meter (always green) and add their effect (§8.6).\n268\t\n269\t**CPU bowlers** use the same pipeline: the AI chooses a delivery and a target (§5.8), its meter press lands at a skill-based offset, and the result goes through the same code.\n270\t\n271\t**Delivery types** are data (§8.4): pace, swing, spin and medium families, each with nominal km/h, length band limits (a bouncer pitches ≥ 140 units from the batter's crease), swing, seam, turn, bounce and dip multipliers, FX and AI weights. The HUD shows the speed after release (\"143 KM/H\") and the delivery name in small type.\n272\t\n273\t**Wides and no-balls:**\n274\t- A **wide** is a ball passing the striker's popping crease more than 26 units to the off side or more than 18 to the leg side of the middle-stump line (x = 0, measured in the striker's frame), without touching the bat or the batter. A ball passing above z = 40 (a head-high bouncer) is also a wide.\n275\t- A **no-ball** comes from an overstep, or a full toss above z = 26 at the crease (\"a beamer\": over the top, the commentators gasp).\n276\t\n277\t**Tests:**\n278\t- meter green → pitches within 3 units of the marker;\n279\t- red tip → `extra:noBall` and the next ball is `freeHit`;"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"df25608b-8d30-41bd-8640-efa7a82527f1","uuid":"b731c1d2-4efa-473d-b494-648babae7b78","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:29:33.381Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261104-062918__xe36ddc/work/PLAN.md","content":"- Dev folder (all working files): `CricketKerfuffle-Cricket-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174825/`, inside the folder that holds TASK.md and this PLAN.md.\n- The single-file game: `CricketKerfuffle-Cricket-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174825.html`, in that same root folder, beside TASK.md.\n- `exit-interview.json`, also in that root folder, beside the .html.\n- If *your* TASK.md gives a different model or timestamp suffix, use your TASK.md's suffix with the same GameTitle and SOURCE tokens.\n- On screen the title is **CRICKET KERFUFFLE**. In the AI Hub catalogue it is `Cricket Kerfuffle`.\n\n### 1.2 Sources to build on\n- **Engine:** my-3D2dge **v0.85.2**, handoff package: `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff` (commit `4390a98` when this plan was written). Copy the whole `dist/handoff/` folder into `<dev>/engine/` and treat it as yours to change (§3.5 lists the planned engine changes).\n- **AI Hub:** Session Protocol v1, client **1.4.0**: `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub`. Download only the files you need with `curl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/<file>`: `README.md`, `session.js`, `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` and `examples/parallel.html`. **Don't `git clone` ai-hub:** a shallow clone took over 5 GB of disk when this plan was written.\n- **Read before coding.** Read these whole: `engine/API.md`, `engine/kits/sports.js` (the closest kit: ball as a Body, aftertouch, formations, a radar, commentary on Dialog's radio, a bot), `engine/docs/ASSETS.md`, `engine/modules/arcade.js` (tables, initials, attract helpers) and the hub `README.md`. In `engine/AI_GUIDE.md`, read these sections: *Standard features*, *Frame order*, *Scenes*, *Camera*, *Players*, *Motion and timing*, *TileMap*, *Humanoid*, *Audio*, *Text and UI*, *Lighting*, *Testing without a browser* and the final *Checklist*. Search `engine/API-INDEX.md` for names; don't read it whole.\n- **Machine etiquette (from TASK.md).** Other agents share the machine.\n  - Read only your own folder.\n  - `/tmp` may be full, so clone into `<dev>/.cache/` or `~/.cache/<unique-name>/`.\n  - When a server is needed, run `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` from the folder to serve, parse the port it prints, and confirm the page is yours (fetch it and check for `<title>Cricket Kerfuffle`).\n  - Stop only the processes you started, by PID. Never use pkill or killall, and never kill whatever holds a port.\n\n### 1.3 Ground rules that shape everything\n- No TODOs, placeholders or stubs: every feature in this plan ships complete. Where the plan offers a simpler fallback (§10), the fallback must be complete too.\n- All audio is synthesized in code: no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish (§5.13).\n- Controller first, for a TV seen from 10 feet away (§5.14 and §5.15). Keyboard and mouse must feel native. Touch-only devices get no touch controls (§5.15.6).\n- The game runs from `file://` with no network requests. The AI Hub client only talks to the hub when the hub opened the page.\n- The performance budget is unlimited (TASK.md), so FPS tuning is out of scope. Still avoid pathological designs, such as re-baking the floor every frame (§5.11).\n\n---\n\n## 2. The game in brief\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Genre | Arcade sports (cricket), over the top, with a comedy story campaign |\n| Perspective | 2.5D in the engine's projected-3D views. A **broadcast camera** (TV-style, behind the bowler, looking down the pitch at the batter) for every delivery. A high **wide camera** that follows the ball round the ground. A side-on **review camera** for third-umpire replays and a **close-up camera** for celebrations. Story scenes use the three-quarter view. |\n| Players | 1 to 4 on one machine: versus, co-op and 2v2, all on one shared screen. 1 to 4 on separate devices through AI Hub sessions (*Super Over Showdown*, parallel mode). |\n| Tone | Village cricket's gentle eccentricity: tea, cake, drizzle, the vicar as umpire, a cow at deep midwicket. Delivered in the voice of the cricket commentary box (digressive, plummy, dry, given to long tangents) and of Wisden-style match reports. Then turned up to arcade bombast: flaming bats, triple-ball bowling machines, sixes through the pub window. |\n| Session | Story campaign of about 40 minutes (5 chapters). A Quick Match takes 4 to 12 minutes. A Super Over Showdown takes 2 minutes. |\n\n**What the player can do:**\n- Bat: aim with the stick or mouse; play a ground, lofted or defensive shot, or leave; charge down the pitch; use a signature special.\n- Run: call runs, send the partner back, dive for the crease.\n- Bowl: pick 1 of 3 deliveries or a special; place the pitching marker; time the release meter; add aftertouch swing or drift; choose the field; bowl over or round the wicket.\n- Field: chase with the active fielder, dive, take catches on a timing ring, throw to either end for run-outs.\n- Review an umpire's decision once per innings.\n- Pick up arcade items, smash stadium targets, survive chaos events, go ON FIRE.\n\n**What the player can't do:** walk freely around the ground, edit teams or players, or play innings longer than 5 overs (no Test matches). In story mode the player always captains Kerfuffle CC. Each screen's help and the Nets mode make these limits clear.\n\n**Modes:** Story (*The Grand Kerfuffle*), Quick Match, Super Over Showdown (bat or bowl; high-score tables), Nets (practice, lessons and the \"Six Machine\" challenge), Versus (local 2–4), AI Hub session (parallel Super Over), plus Attract mode, Dev mode and About/Credits.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Architecture\n\n### 3.1 Stack\n- **my-3D2dge v0.85.2**, the full engine (`engine/my-3d2dge.js`, every module built in). Use these parts:\n  - `E.Game` scenes, boot steps, the standard pause menu, settings, controls, full screen and saves;\n  - `E.TileMap` for the ground;\n  - custom `E.View`s;\n  - `E.Humanoid` rigs, `E.Attack` specs and `E.ACTIONS`;\n  - `E.Body`, particles, lights and weather;\n  - `E.Dialog`, `E.Menu`, `E.font`, `E.ui`;\n  - `game.script`, `game.tween`, `E.Path`;\n  - the `arcade` module (`E.Arcade` tables and initials), the `story` module (`E.Story` for cutscene data) and the `songs` module (jingles);\n  - the chip synth `game.audio`, plus our own Web Audio voice synth on the engine's audio context.\n- **AI Hub session client 1.4.0**, pasted unmodified.\n- **Node 18+** for the build and the headless tests. **Playwright 1.63.0** (pinned by `engine/package.json`) for the browser checks and the AI Hub harness.\n- Nothing else. No libraries, no CDN, no fetch.\n\n### 3.2 Folder layout (inside `<dev>`)\n```\nengine/                 copy of dist/handoff (modified: see 3.5 and engine/CHANGES-KERFUFFLE.md)\nvendor/aihub/           session.js (unmodified), harness.html, bridge.js, round.js, README.md\nsrc/                    the game: plain script fragments, concatenated in name order into one IIFE\n  00-head.js            'use strict'; const E = My3D2dge; shared helpers (clamp, lerp, ...)\n  01-const.js           units, geometry, enums (4)\n  02-tuning.js          TUNING registry: defaults, difficulty multipliers, live get/set, persistence (5.19)\n  03-rng.js             seeded streams: play, cosmetic, hub (3.4)\n  04-bus.js             event bus: on(type, fn), emit(type, data); a 200-entry event log for dev mode\n  05-store.js           profile: campaign progress, stars, unlocks, settings mirror, high-score ids\n  06-assets-store.js    the few store entries used, cut by engine/tools/asset-set.mjs (6.3)\n  07-assets-kerfuffle.js our new shapes and materials, as a standalone set file (6.5)\n  10-data-teams.js      teams and players (8.2)\n  11-data-grounds.js    ground recipes (8.3)\n  12-data-deliveries.js delivery catalogue (8.4)\n  13-data-shots.js      shot catalogue and the resolver table (8.5)\n  14-data-specials.js   signature specials (8.6)\n  15-data-arcade.js     items, stadium targets, chaos events (8.7)\n  16-data-story.js      chapters, scenes, beats, goals (8.8)\n  17-data-lines.js      commentary lines, match-report templates, UI copy (8.9)\n  18-data-music.js      songs and stings (8.10)\n  20-ball.js            ball physics and the trajectory predictor (5.1)\n  21-bowling.js         delivery choice, marker, meter, release, aftertouch (5.2)\n  22-batting.js         shot resolver, timing, contact, edges (5.3)\n  23-running.js         running between wickets (5.4)\n  24-fielding.js        fielders, interception, catches, throws, keeper (5.5)\n  25-umpire.js          decisions, LBW, run-out, stumping, review (5.6)\n  26-rules.js           match state, laws, scoring, scorecard (5.7)\n  27-ai.js              CPU batting, bowling, captain, fielding, autopilot (5.8)\n  28-hype.js            Hype meter, ON FIRE, specials runtime (5.9)\n  29-chaos.js           items, targets, chaos events, weather, time of day (5.10)\n  30-ground.js          TileMap builder from a ground recipe, floor texture, stands, crowd, panoramas (6.3)\n  31-cast.js            rig factory: kits, gear, bat, faces, portraits (6.4)\n  32-anim.js            pose library: stance, shots, bowling action, fielding, umpire signals (7)\n  33-fx.js              trails, flames, stumps and bails, splinters, fireworks, confetti (6.6)\n  34-camera.js          views and the camera director (5.11)\n  35-replay.js          recorder, Owl-Eye replay, third-umpire screen (5.11)\n  40-audio.js           SFX voices, crowd engine, music glue, stings (5.13)\n  41-voice.js           gibberish speech synth (5.13.3)\n  42-commentary.js      commentary director and captions (5.12)\n  43-glyphs.js          button and key icons, device-following prompts (5.14.3)\n  44-hud.js             match HUD (5.15.2)\n  45-ui.js              10-foot widgets: big menus, cards, panels, sliders (5.15)\n  50-title.js 51-attract.js 52-front.js (main menu, setup, versus lobby) 53-story.js (map, cutscenes)\n  54-match.js (gameplay scene) 55-results.js 56-nets.js 57-showdown.js 58-about.js\n  60-hub.js             AI Hub session flow (5.17)\n  70-dev.js             dev panel (5.19)\n  80-debug.js           game.debug: snapshot, force*, bot hooks (11.2)\n  99-start.js           new E.Game(...), scenes, boot steps, game.start\ntools/build.mjs         concatenate src -> dist/game.js; write dist/index.html (dev page) and the single-file game\ntools/serve.mjs         start python3 http.server on port 0 at 127.0.0.1; return { port, pid, stop() }\ntest/run.mjs            THE one test command (11.1)\ntest/suites/*.mjs       the suites\ntest/hub/               harness files + the built game, served for the harness run\ndist/                   game.js, index.html (loads ../engine/my-3d2dge.js and game.js: quick iteration)\nDESIGN-NOTES.md         short: how to add a team, a ground, a chapter, a delivery, a shot, a line\n```\n\n### 3.3 How the code is organized\n- **Simulation and presentation are separate.** The match simulation lives in `20–29`. It is pure and deterministic: fixed steps of 1/120 s, seeded RNG, no drawing and no audio. Presentation lives in `30–45`: it reads the simulation's state and listens on the event bus. Tests drive the simulation headless. The attract mode and the AI drive it through the same input interface a player uses.\n- **Registries.** All content is data keyed by id: teams, players, grounds, deliveries, shots, specials, items, targets, events, chapters and lines. Systems look things up by id, and content never edits system code. Adding a ground means a new recipe in `11-data-grounds.js` plus any new shapes. `DESIGN-NOTES.md` says how, for each kind of content.\n- **Event bus.** The rules emit events: `ball:released`, `ball:pitched`, `bat:contact {timing, shot}`, `ball:edge`, `ball:beaten`, `runs {n}`, `boundary {4|6}`, `wicket {how}`, `catch:dropped`, `appeal`, `decision {out, review}`, `extra {kind}`, `over:end`, `innings:end`, `match:end {result}`, `hype:full`, `fire:on|off`, `special {id}`, `item {id}`, `target:hit {id}`, `chaos {id}`, `rain:start|end`, `review:start|result`, `milestone {fifty|hattrick|maiden}`. Commentary, crowd, HUD, camera, Hype, achievements and the coverage tracker (§11) all subscribe.\n- **TUNING.** One registry of the live gameplay variables. Systems read `T('ballSpeed')` at each use and never cache it, so dev sliders take effect at once.\n- **The delivery director.** One state machine runs each ball (§5.7.3): `setup → runup → release → flight → inPlay → dead → between`. Each state has explicit exits and a timeout, so play can never stall.\n- **Scenes** (`game.go(name, data)`): `title`, `attract`, `front` (main menu, mode select, setup, versus lobby), `storymap`, `cutscene`, `match` (pausable), `results`, `nets` (pausable), `showdown` (pausable), `about`, `hubcard` and `hubround` (pausable). Story, Quick Match, Showdown, Nets and the attract mode all run inside the `match` scene machinery, using a `MatchConfig` (§5.7.1).\n\n### 3.4 Determinism and randomness\n- `RNG.play`: gameplay randomness (seam, AI errors, fielding). Seeded per match from `MatchConfig.seed`.\n- `RNG.cosmetic`: crowd, particles and commentary-line choice, so gameplay never depends on them.\n- In AI Hub sessions, the over's deliveries come from `AIHub.rng('over')` and each ball's fielding stream from `AIHub.rng('field' + n)`, so every copy faces identical balls whatever the player does (§5.17).\n- Never call `Math.random` in simulation code. It is fine for engine-internal cosmetics.\n\n### 3.5 Planned engine changes\nMake each change in `engine/my-3d2dge.js`. Log every one in `engine/CHANGES-KERFUFFLE.md`; About/Credits and the exit interview report them.\n1. **Cricket bat:** a new Humanoid `weapon: 'bat'`. Add it next to the `o.weapon === 'sword'` branch in the HD draw, which uses `ops.push({ d, f })` with `J.handR`, `J.bladeDir` and `o.bladeLen`. It is a flat willow blade: length `o.batLen` (default 15 units: 5 handle, 10 blade), width 1.7 units, a darker edge line, a toe, and a coloured sticker band. Grip in `colors.grip`, blade in `colors.willow`. Give it a two-handed grip: the left hand IK'd to the handle, 2.5 units above the right hand (`E.ik3` from the left shoulder), applied in the draw pose. Add a smear for swings.\n2. **Cricket gear:**\n   - `pads: true`: white leg guards over the shins and knees, with buckles in `colors.trim`;\n   - a `brim` hat style (umpire's and sun hats);\n   - `hat: { style: 'helmet', grille: true }`: a batting helmet with a face grille;\n   - `gloves: 'batting' | 'keeper'` for bigger hands;\n   - `face: { moustache: 'handlebar' | 'walrus' | null, beard: 'full' | 'goatee' | null, glasses: true }`.\n3. **Audio voice bus:** add `_voiceBus` beside `_sfxBus` and `_musBus`, a `voiceVolume` setting in the Sound group (label \"Voices\"), and `audio.bus(name)` returning the sfx, music or voice gain node, so the game's own Web Audio nodes follow the player's volumes.\n4. **Button symbols:** in the standard settings table (`glyphs`, around line 2999), set the default to `'playstation'` and the options to `[['playstation','PlayStation'],['xbox','Xbox (A B X Y)']]`.\n5. **Hub's Create + Options:** while `game.hubActive` is set and Pad8 (Create) is held, Pad9 (Options) must not open the pause menu.\n6. **Floor bakes:** `TileMap._floor` keeps at most 6 baked floors (it clears the cache past that). Raise the limit to 10, so the four match cameras and the story views never re-bake (§5.11).\n7. Any engine bug found along the way: fix it, and add one line about it to the log.\n\n### 3.6 Build and bundle (`node tools/build.mjs`)\n1. Concatenate `src/*.js` in name order inside `(() => { ... })();`, giving `dist/game.js`. Fail on a syntax error: run `new Function` on it in Node.\n2. Write `dist/index.html`, which loads `../engine/my-3d2dge.js` and `game.js` as separate scripts (fast iteration).\n3. Write the single file `../CricketKerfuffle-Cricket-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174825.html`, in exactly this order:\n   - `<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\">`;\n   - **the session client** (`vendor/aihub/session.js`, byte-for-byte) as the first `<script>`;\n   - viewport meta, `<title>Cricket Kerfuffle</title>`;\n   - the hub tags (§5.17.1) and catalogue tags `hub:type`=`Game`, `hub:players`=`1-4`;\n   - the page CSS (from `engine/game/index.html`), `</head><body>`;\n   - the engine's boot splash markup (from `engine/game/index.html`, title CRICKET KERFUFFLE);\n   - `<canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas>`;\n   - the engine inlined in a `<script>`, then `dist/game.js` inlined in a `<script>`.\n   Escape any `</script>` inside the inlined code.\n4. Print the size and the SHA-1 of the client block. The bundle test (§11) checks the block is identical to `vendor/aihub/session.js`.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Units, coordinates and constants (`01-const.js`)\n\n- **Engine convention:** 16 world units = 1 m (docs/ASSETS.md). x east (screen right), y south (screen down in yaw-0 views), z up.\n- **The pitch** runs along x = 0. The **striker's stumps are at y = 0** (the far end, top of the broadcast screen). The **bowler's stumps are at y = 320** (20 m; the near end, bottom of the screen). The striker's popping crease is at y = 20 and the bowler's at y = 300. Return creases are at x = ±21. The pitch strip is 48 units wide (3 m).\n- The camera always looks from behind the current bowler, so the bowling end is always at +y. At each change of ends, the batters swap logical places and the field is recomputed relative to the new striker. Visually, the \"far-end panorama\" (§6.3) switches to the other end's.\n- **Stumps:** physics box x ∈ [−3, 3], z ∈ [0, 12], depth 1. Drawn slightly larger for readability: 6 units wide, 12.5 tall, with two bails.\n- **Ball:** physics radius 0.6 units. Drawn as a 4×4 px sprite at minimum, with a 2 px shadow disc, a trail and a glow at night. Leather red `#b8202a` with a cream seam, white at night, pink at the Megadome.\n- **Players:** Humanoid size 1.15–1.25 (about 28–30 units tall), so `E.HEIGHTS` defaults hold.\n- **Batter's frame (handedness):** angles are measured from \"straight\" (toward the bowler, +y), positive toward the **off side**. A right-hander (RHB) faces −x in stance, so the off side is −x (screen left in the broadcast view). For a left-hander, everything mirrors (x → −x). Store `hand = +1` (RHB) or `−1` (LHB). The world direction for a batter-frame angle θ is `[−hand·sin θ, cos θ]`.\n- **Boundary:** an ellipse per ground (rx × ry, about 560–760 units, i.e. 35–48 m) centred on the pitch midpoint (0, 160), scaled by `boundaryScale` *(T)*.\n- **Physics constants:**\n\n  | Constant | Value |\n  |---|---|\n  | `gravity` *(T)* | 160 u/s² (real) |\n  | `ballSpeed` *(T)* | scale 0.62 on nominal pace (a \"140 km/h\" ball travels at 386 u/s and reaches the bat in about 0.75 s) |\n  | `pitchBounce` *(T)* | restitution 0.55 (times the ground's pitch factor) |\n  | Outfield rolling decay | exp(−k·dt), k = 0.55 (ground factor; wet ×1.6) |\n  | Grass bounce for hit balls | restitution 0.42 |\n  | `runSpeed` *(T)* | 120 u/s |\n  | `fieldSpeed` *(T)* | 105 u/s |\n  | `throwSpeed` *(T)* | 420 u/s |\n\n  At these values a single takes about 2.9 s, and a fielder 30 m away gathering in 1.2 s makes a close run-out.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Systems and mechanics\n\n### 5.1 Ball physics and predictor (`20-ball.js`)\n- **State:** `{ x, y, z, vx, vy, vz, spin (rad/s about z, signed), swingA (lateral accel), age, phase: 'held'|'delivery'|'hit'|'rolling'|'dead', pitched, bounces, lastTouch: {kind, id}, carried (hit and not yet bounced), trail[] }`.\n- **Step (1/120 s), in order:**\n  1. Gravity: vz −= g·dt.\n  2. While `phase === 'delivery'` and not yet pitched, add swing: a lateral acceleration perpendicular to the ball's ground velocity, `swingA · min(1, age/0.35)` (late swing).\n  3. Drift for spin.\n  4. Drag: v ×= (1 − 0.02·dt).\n  5. Integrate.\n  6. Ground contact at z ≤ 0:\n     - **On the pitch** (|x| ≤ 24 and y in −20..340), for a delivery: vz' = −vz · pitchBounce · ground.bounce · delivery.bounce. Horizontal speed × (0.86 − spinGrip). Turn the ground velocity by `turn° · ground.turn · T('spin')` plus the ball's stored seam deviation `ball.seamDev`. That deviation is drawn once **at release** from RNG.play (normal, σ = `seam` *(T)* · ground.seam · delivery.seam degrees) and kept on the ball. Emit `ball:pitched`.\n     - **On grass** (any ball after the bat): bounce at 0.42; below 25 u/s vertically it becomes `rolling`, with decay k.\n  7. Boundary check (§5.7.4).\n  8. Target and item checks (§5.10).\n  9. Stumps check (§5.6).\n- **Solving a delivery** (`solveDelivery(release, target, kmh, deliveryType)`): given the release point (x = ±8 for over or round the wicket, y = 300, z = 34), the pitch target (tx, ty) and the speed, find the initial velocity that lands at the target *without* swing, using `E.arc`-style closed-form projectile maths with the delivery's dip. Swing and aftertouch then move the real pitching point. That is the point of swing, and what the batter must read.\n- **Predictor** (`predict(ballState, seconds, opts)`): runs the same step function on a copy and returns sampled points `{t, x, y, z, phase}` every 1/120 s. It is used for:\n  - the batting timing (when the ball reaches the contact plane);\n  - the AI's reads;\n  - fielder interception;\n  - the landing point of a catch;\n  - LBW (the path continued past the pad);\n  - the Owl-Eye tube.\n\n  The step function draws no random numbers (the seam deviation is already on the ball), so the prediction equals reality exactly. The AI adds its own perception error on top.\n- **Tests (unit-ball):**\n  - 50 random solved deliveries without swing pitch within 1 unit of the target;\n  - an outswinger to a right-hander lands on the off side of the target (−x) and an inswinger on the leg side;\n  - a leg break pitched on middle turns toward −x for a right-hander;\n  - the predictor matches the stepped simulation within 0.01 units over 3 s;\n  - rolling balls stop;\n  - a ball dropped from z = 50 bounces lower each time.\n\n### 5.2 Bowling (`21-bowling.js`)\n**The flow for a human bowler:**\n1. **Between balls**:\n   - R1 (Space) cycles the field preset (Attacking, Balanced, Defensive, or Spin attack for spinners);\n   - L1 (Shift) switches over or round the wicket;\n   - the stick, WASD or the mouse moves the **pitch marker**: a ring on the pitch, clamped to y ∈ [20, 230] and |x| ≤ 30, with labelled length bands *yorker / full / good / short / bouncer* drawn faintly on the pitch the first few overs;\n   - the delivery panel at the bottom shows the bowler's 3 deliveries on ✕ □ ○ and the special on △ (greyed out until Hype is full).\n2. Pressing a delivery button starts the **run-up**: pace bowlers run 2.0 s, medium 1.5 s, spinners 0.9 s. The marker can still move during the run-up, at half speed.\n3. **The release meter**: during the last 0.7 s, a needle sweeps a bar left → right → left at `meterSpeed` *(T)* (1.4 Hz). The centre zone (width `meterSweet` *(T)* 0.18 of the bar, scaled by bowler skill) is green. Press any delivery button or click (✕/J/LMB) to release:\n   - **green**: accuracy scatter ±2 units, pace and spin 100–105%;\n   - **amber**: scatter ±10, 90%;\n   - **red edge**: scatter ±18, 80%;\n   - **the far-right red tip** (the last 6%) is an **overstep**: a no-ball and a FREE HIT;\n   - **no press**: auto-release at the end with an amber result.\n4. **Aftertouch**: for 0.25 s after release, the stick's horizontal push adds swing (seamers) or drift (spinners), up to the delivery's allowance ±0.6. The mouse's horizontal movement does the same.\n5. **Specials** (△ when Hype is full) skip the meter (always green) and add their effect (§8.6).\n\n**CPU bowlers** use the same pipeline: the AI chooses a delivery and a target (§5.8), its meter press lands at a skill-based offset, and the result goes through the same code.\n\n**Delivery types** are data (§8.4): pace, swing, spin and medium families, each with nominal km/h, length band limits (a bouncer pitches ≥ 140 units from the batter's crease), swing, seam, turn, bounce and dip multipliers, FX and AI weights. The HUD shows the speed after release (\"143 KM/H\") and the delivery name in small type.\n\n**Wides and no-balls:**\n- A **wide** is a ball passing the striker's popping crease more than 26 units to the off side or more than 18 to the leg side of the middle-stump line (x = 0, measured in the striker's frame), without touching the bat or the batter. A ball passing above z = 40 (a head-high bouncer) is also a wide.\n- A **no-ball** comes from an overstep, or a full toss above z = 26 at the crease (\"a beamer\": over the top, the commentators gasp).\n\n**Tests:**\n- meter green → pitches within 3 units of the marker;\n- red tip → `extra:noBall` and the next ball is `freeHit`;","numLines":260,"startLine":20,"totalLines":1435}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01XMRgfjporPoJirjw4xneqX","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
