{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi2NJb8FAzo7auZutFyN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cricket Kerfuffle is built and bundled, and `exit-interview.json` parses and has every field filled. The single file is `CricketKerfuffle-Cricket-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-062918.html` in the root, 1.3 MB, with no network requests. All 68 test checks pass, including the AI Hub harness in a real browser.\n\nI built a reduced slice of the 148 KB plan, not all of it. TASK.md's requirements are in; the plan's review/third-umpire replay and nets practice mode are not. I did not play it by hand with a controller. Visuals were checked from Playwright screenshots, and the headless playthrough drove it with scripted input.\n\n**What's in it**\n- **Modes:** Story (three chapters with cutscenes), Quick Match, Super Over (score table with initials), Versus (two players, one screen), and the AI Hub parallel round.\n- **Attract mode:** after 10 idle seconds on the title, a looping AI-played match starts, and any input exits it.\n- **Dev mode:** the pause menu has a \"Dev mode\" item with live sliders for the balance variables, a reset-all, and test tools (score a six, take a wicket, spawn a cow, rain, golden ball, god mode, slow motion).\n- **Controls:** prompts follow the last device used (PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox as a setting), menus take mouse hover and click, and a touch-only device sees \"Controller or keyboard required\".\n- **Audio and tone:** all audio is code, with Animalese-style gibberish voices for the commentary and cutscenes. The writing is village-cricket commentary box.\n- **Engine changes:** five, logged in `engine/CHANGES-KERFUFFLE.md`. I added a cricket bat weapon, a voice audio bus, PlayStation as the default glyphs, and a larger floor cache. I also fixed the engine's weapon whitelist, which was silently swapping the bat for a sword.\n\n**Balance caveat:** CPU batters rarely take singles, so a CPU innings is mostly dots and boundaries. Scores average about 13 runs in a 2-over innings.\n\n## Source Information\n- Date: 2026-11-04 06:29:18\n- Source: cricket (over the top arcade style)\n- Plan: PLAN.md (written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort), built in a reduced scope, TASK.md winning where they differ\n- Genre and Perspective: arcade sports (cricket) with a comedy story campaign; 2.5D three-quarter broadcast camera behind the bowler, with a wide follow cut once the ball is hit\n- Tech: my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (modified); plain JavaScript in one IIFE bundled into a single HTML file; AI Hub session client 1.4.0 (unmodified); Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 for the tests\n- Art Direction: the engine's native pixel-art-from-3D-rigs look, with a new cricket bat, a procedural ground texture, and buildings, cow, crowd and stumps drawn in code\n- Animation: the engine's Humanoid rig with E.MOVES, poses and run cycles, driven by the match simulation\n- Audio: all in code, with chip-synth effects, five songs, and a Web Audio formant synth for gibberish voices; no audio files\n- Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1, parallel mode, a seeded one-over Super Over with scores reported to the hub; passes the hub harness\n- Testing: headless only (simulation checks, a scripted playthrough of every mode, a Playwright smoke run, the AI Hub harness)\n- Research: none beyond the engine docs, the AI Hub README and the plan\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **Issues caught:** about ten real ones. A rolling ball re-bounced every step, and a falsy batter id (0) meant balls were never judged boundaries, so play stalled for 28 s a ball. CPU batters ran into certain run-outs, and there were far too many wides, sixes and deliveries that went over the stumps. The engine rejected the bat weapon, there was no in-memory save fallback, the camera cropped the striker, and some font glyphs drew as question marks.\n- **Game breaking:** three. They were the falsy batter id, the engine whitelist swapping the bat for a sword, and camera framing that hid the batter behind the HUD.\n- **Most helpful test:** running 30 complete AI-versus-AI matches headless and printing the outcome distribution. It found the stalled balls and every balance problem within seconds.\n- **Least helpful test:** a Playwright autopilot that pressed keys through polling. Browser latency meant it couldn't hit the timing window, so it only proved the page didn't crash.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** Humanoid rigs with ready-made moves and poses gave animated characters for free. The Game shell and `game.advance` / `game.debug` made headless scene testing easy.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong:** yes, three times. `setZoom(1)` is not the view scale (the three-quarter base is 1.5 and zoom can't go below 0.5), so my first framing was far too tight. The bat weapon was silently swapped for a sword by a whitelist. `E.store` needs localStorage and doesn't work in Node.\n- **Most important thing I built:** the whole cricket simulation (ball physics with a predictor, the laws, running, fielder AI, hype and ON FIRE, chaos), plus the HUD, the 10-foot menus, the formant voice synth and the hub round.\n- **Hardest part:** the camera. That meant view scale versus zoom versus the buffer size on an expanded 427×240 screen, and fitting a 10-foot UI in the pixel font at that size.\n- **Engine changes:** a cricket bat weapon, an audio voice bus with a Voices setting, PlayStation as the default glyphs with an Xbox option, and a floor cache of ten instead of six. I also fixed the weapon whitelist.\n- **Change one thing:** a wider documented zoom range, with each view's real pixels-per-unit stated beside the presets.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** the concrete deliverables, the testing guidance (one command, only failures printed), and the exit interview. The plan was rich with ideas to draw on.\n- **Confusing:** the plan is about 148 KB and asks for far more than one session can build, so how much of it to honour was unclear. Its file and folder names differ from TASK.md's, and I had to resolve that myself.\n- **Hardest thing:** scoping a feature-complete slice (story, four modes, attract, dev mode, local and online multiplayer, Animalese voices, 10-foot UI) and verifying it without a human watching the screen.\n- **What I'd change:** tier the plan into must, should and could, say which names and numbers TASK.md overrides, and give a small reference game to calibrate scope.\n\nEverything is in the dev folder `CricketKerfuffle-Cricket-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-062918/`. Run `node tools/build.mjs && node test/run.mjs` there to rebuild the bundle and rerun the tests."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1014,"cache_read_input_tokens":416200,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1014},"output_tokens":760,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"df25608b-8d30-41bd-8640-efa7a82527f1","uuid":"553ff57b-717b-49b4-a8d4-7045ca8d87fb","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:12:26.065Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi2NJ9aCySUHWJQjpwXw"}
